T23.01|A mutual fund or exchange-traded fund pools investors' money into a portfolio under a stated mandate. Investors own fund units rather than directly choosing each underlying holding. Structure, management method and economic exposure are separate questions: an ETF can be active, and a mutual fund can track an index.~~Fund returns reflect underlying investments less expenses, trading costs and any tracking effects. Exchange trading introduces a market price that can differ from net asset value; ordinary open-end dealing generally uses the applicable calculated NAV.|Mutual fund: Pooled investment with fund-level dealing rules.;;ETF: Units traded on an exchange.;;Underlying exposure: Securities or assets that drive returns.|An illustrative fund has assets of ₹110 lakh, liabilities of ₹10 and 10 lakh units.~~Net assets are ₹100 lakh.;;NAV is ₹10 per unit.;;An exchange price of ₹10.20 represents a 2% premium to that NAV.~~The portfolio value and trading price need not coincide.~~Dealing frequency, liquidity and authorised-participant mechanisms depend on the structure.|Read the mandate.;;Compare fees and underlying exposure.;;Check dealing and price-versus-NAV risks.
T23.02|Open-end funds can issue and redeem units under their dealing rules, so their size changes with investor flows. Closed-end funds typically have a more fixed capital base and investors exit through secondary trading or defined arrangements. These structures affect liquidity and portfolio management, especially when underlying assets are hard to sell.~~A liquid-looking quoted unit is not evidence that the underlying portfolio is liquid. Redemption limits, gates and discounts can become important during stress.|Open-end: Capital changes through issuance and redemption.;;Closed-end: Capital generally fixed for a defined structure.;;Secondary market discount: Price below underlying NAV.|A closed-end vehicle reports ₹100 NAV per unit but trades at ₹85.~~Discount is (100 − 85) ÷ 100 = 15%.;;Buying at ₹85 exposes the investor to asset performance and discount changes.;;If NAV stays ₹100 but price falls to ₹80, the investor still loses money.~~Underlying value alone does not determine realised return.~~NAV may itself be estimated for illiquid assets.|Inspect redemption rights.;;Assess underlying asset liquidity.;;Separate NAV changes from discount changes.
T23.03|An index fund follows a rules-based benchmark; an active fund selects exposures to pursue an objective that may differ from that benchmark. Neither label specifies the asset class, risk level or fee automatically. Index construction itself determines concentration and factor exposure.~~Evaluate active results after costs and against an appropriate benchmark over a relevant horizon. Index funds can have tracking differences, while active outperformance may reflect luck or an unrecognised risk exposure.|Index replication: Holds benchmark constituents closely.;;Sampling: Uses representative holdings to approximate exposure.;;Active selection: Deviates intentionally under a mandate.|A benchmark returns 8%; an index fund returns 7.7%; an active fund returns 9% before a 1.5% fee.~~Index tracking difference is −0.3 percentage points.;;Approximate active net return is 7.5%.;;Compare risk and exposure before ranking performance.~~Gross outperformance can disappear after fees.~~Exact fee deductions compound through time rather than always subtracting mechanically.|Identify benchmark rules.;;Use net returns.;;Examine persistence, risk and concentration.
T23.04|NAV is net fund assets divided by units outstanding. Expense ratios describe recurring operating charges relative to assets, but do not necessarily capture every investor cost. Tracking difference is the fund's return minus benchmark return over a period; tracking error measures variability in those differences.~~Cash balances, withholding taxes, replication, rebalancing and trading can affect index tracking in addition to the stated fee. Compare funds on consistent currency, reinvestment and tax conventions.|NAV: Accounting value per unit.;;Expense ratio: Recurring fund costs relative to assets.;;Tracking difference: Return gap over a specified period.|A fund has ₹200 lakh assets, ₹4 liabilities and 20 lakh units; its annual return is 9.4% against 10%.~~NAV is ₹196 ÷ 20 = ₹9.80.;;Return difference is −0.6 percentage points.;;A 0.3% expense ratio alone does not explain the entire gap.~~Value, expenses and realised tracking describe different aspects.~~The remaining difference requires reconciliation, not an assumed explanation.|Check total costs.;;Match benchmark return conventions.;;Reconcile tracking sources over time.
T23.05|Manager due diligence examines whether a repeatable investment process, capable team and sound operations support the mandate. Historical returns are an outcome to investigate, not a substitute for understanding decisions, risk and custody. A strong investment thesis cannot compensate for weak asset controls.~~Selection includes portfolio fit: a good standalone fund may duplicate exposures already held. Capacity, key-person dependence and changes in incentives can alter future performance.|Investment review: Philosophy, process and portfolio construction.;;Operational review: Custody, valuation and controls.;;Business review: Ownership, staffing and financial stability.|Two funds both earned 12%, but one used substantial leverage and illiquid holdings while the other stayed within a liquid mandate.~~Compare exposures and drawdowns.;;Verify independent valuation and custody.;;Evaluate whether either fits the investor's liquidity needs.~~Equal historical returns do not establish equal suitability.~~A short record cannot demonstrate a durable skill advantage.|Investigate the process.;;Verify operational safeguards.;;Assess portfolio overlap and liquidity.
T23.06|Portfolio management services and alternative investment funds describe different arrangements for professional investment management. A managed account may hold assets for an individual client, while a pooled fund combines capital under shared documents. The precise regulatory meanings of PMS and AIF vary by jurisdiction.~~Personalisation, custody, fees, leverage and redemption terms must be read from the actual mandate. A sophisticated label does not remove investment or operational risk.|Managed account: Portfolio held under a client mandate.;;Pooled alternative fund: Investors participate in a common vehicle.;;Discretionary mandate: Manager makes decisions within agreed limits.|An illustrative managed account starts with ₹100 lakh and earns ₹8 before a ₹1.5 fee.~~Net gain before other costs and taxes is ₹6.5.;;Inspect whether performance fees add another charge.;;Compare custody and liquidity with a pooled alternative.~~Structure changes control and access as well as costs.~~No eligibility or minimum-investment rule is assumed here.|Read local definitions.;;Inspect custody and fee schedules.;;Compare redemption and leverage terms.
T23.07|Hedge funds use flexible strategies that may include short selling, derivatives, leverage and concentrated positions. The name does not guarantee that market risk is hedged or returns are positive. Strategy and implementation determine exposure.~~Apparent smoothness can reflect illiquid valuations; leverage and crowded exits can magnify losses. Assess net and gross exposure, financing terms, liquidity and sources of profit together.|Long-short equity: Combines selected long and short positions.;;Event-driven: Trades outcomes such as restructurings or transactions.;;Macro/relative value: Trades broad drivers or pricing relationships.|A fund owns ₹100 lakh of shares and shorts ₹60.~~Net exposure is ₹40.;;Gross exposure is ₹160.;;If longs fall 10% and shorts rise 5%, loss is ₹10 + ₹3 = ₹13 before costs.~~Low net exposure does not imply low total risk.~~Borrowing costs, short squeezes and differing betas add further risk.|Identify the return source.;;Measure gross leverage.;;Stress funding and exit liquidity.
T23.08|Private equity invests in companies outside ordinary public-market trading, often with active governance and a multi-year exit plan. Buyout returns can come from operating growth, cash generation, debt repayment and valuation changes; financial leverage magnifies both gains and losses.~~An exit assumption is not cash received. Fees, follow-on capital and the timing of distributions matter to investor outcomes.|Buyout: Acquires substantial control, often using debt.;;Growth equity: Funds expansion of an established business.;;Secondary investment: Buys existing private interests.|A buyer pays enterprise value ₹100 lakh using debt ₹60 and equity ₹40; later EV is ₹120 and debt ₹40.~~Exit equity is ₹80.;;Equity multiple is 80 ÷ 40 = 2×.;;Attribute ₹20 to higher EV and ₹20 to lower debt.~~Both business value and financing drive the equity result.~~The example excludes fees, interest timing and interim cash flows.|Separate operating and leverage effects.;;Test exit valuation.;;Review governance and liquidity terms.
T23.09|Venture capital finances young businesses with uncertain outcomes; growth equity generally funds more developed businesses seeking expansion. A few large successes can dominate a venture portfolio, so individual valuation and portfolio construction are linked. Funding milestones reduce uncertainty but do not guarantee product-market fit.~~Ownership dilution, liquidation preferences and future capital needs can materially change the investor's share of exit proceeds.|Early-stage venture: Backs uncertain product and market development.;;Late-stage venture: Funds scaling before a mature exit.;;Growth equity: Supports expansion of an established model.|A fund invests ₹1 lakh in each of ten startups; one returns ₹20 and the others return zero.~~Total invested is ₹10.;;Gross proceeds are ₹20.;;Gross multiple is 2× before fees and time effects.~~A portfolio can succeed despite many failures.~~This constructed distribution is not a prediction or evidence of typical returns.|Model follow-on funding.;;Read preference and dilution terms.;;Evaluate outcomes across the whole portfolio.
T23.10|Private credit supplies negotiated loans outside standard public bond markets. Direct lending commonly funds companies through privately arranged debt, with returns from contractual interest, fees and principal repayment. Illiquidity and bespoke terms require careful underwriting.~~A high coupon may compensate for credit loss, leverage, limited exit options or structural weakness. Covenants provide information and negotiating rights but cannot create repayment cash by themselves.|Senior secured: Higher-ranking claim with collateral rights.;;Unitranche: Combines risk layers in one financing package.;;Mezzanine: Junior debt with higher risk and possible equity features.|A ₹100 lakh loan pays 12% interest; default later yields ₹70 principal recovery.~~Annual promised interest is ₹12.;;Principal loss is ₹30.;;Even receiving one full year's interest leaves ₹18 short of initial principal.~~Coupon must be weighed against expected loss and timing.~~Recovery depends on enforceable rights, costs and business value.|Underwrite cash repayment.;;Review seniority and collateral.;;Stress covenant and refinancing outcomes.
T23.11|Distressed investing buys claims where financial difficulty or restructuring creates uncertain recovery. Special situations invest around events that may change value or ownership, such as spin-offs or litigation outcomes. Legal priority, information and timing can matter as much as ordinary earnings forecasts.~~A cheap price relative to face value does not imply a bargain: face value is a contractual amount, while recovery reflects the assets available and claim rights.|Distressed debt: Claims with impaired repayment prospects.;;Restructuring equity: Ownership received through reorganisation.;;Event opportunity: Investment tied to a defined catalyst.|A claim with ₹100 face value costs ₹40 and eventually pays ₹55.~~Cash gain is ₹15.;;Multiple is 55 ÷ 40 = 1.375×.;;If recovery instead is ₹25, loss is ₹15.~~The investment is a recovery estimate, not a face-value discount alone.~~Annualised return also depends on duration and legal costs.|Map the claim waterfall.;;Use recovery scenarios.;;Allow for delays and litigation costs.
T23.12|Property and infrastructure vehicles pool income-producing assets and financing. REITs and infrastructure investment trusts can provide traded exposure to underlying cash flows, but structure, permitted assets and distribution rules differ by country. Distributions may include income or other components and should be reconciled to cash generation.~~Interest rates, asset valuation, tenant or concession quality and leverage affect both unit prices and operating outcomes.|REIT: Vehicle principally holding real estate exposure.;;Infrastructure trust: Vehicle holding eligible infrastructure exposure.;;Private property fund: Negotiated, often less liquid pooled structure.|An illustrative property vehicle collects rent ₹12 lakh, pays operating costs ₹4 and interest ₹3.~~NOI before financing is ₹8.;;Cash after interest is ₹5 before capex and other items.;;A ₹6 distribution would require an additional source.~~Distribution yield alone does not establish sustainability.~~Accounting income and distributable cash use different adjustments.|Reconcile distributions to cash.;;Review leverage and asset concentration.;;Check local vehicle rules.
T23.13|Commodities provide exposure to physical goods whose prices reflect supply, demand, inventories and market structure. Ownership through physical assets, producers or futures produces different risks. Gold's role differs from industrial inputs, but neither has a guaranteed protective return.~~Futures-based returns include spot-price movement, contract roll effects and collateral returns. Producer equity also contains management, costs, leverage and country exposure.|Physical holding: Direct exposure with storage and custody costs.;;Commodity futures: Contract exposure with roll and margin effects.;;Producer equity: Business exposure linked to commodity economics.|A metal price rises 10%, while a producer sells 100 units with unit price rising from ₹10 to ₹11 and cost staying ₹8.~~Initial operating contribution is ₹200.;;New contribution is ₹300.;;Contribution rises 50%, before other changes.~~Producer earnings can be more sensitive than the commodity price.~~Volumes, costs and hedges may reverse this simplified effect.|Distinguish instrument from commodity.;;Inspect futures roll costs.;;Stress producer costs and leverage.
T23.14|Infrastructure funds invest in long-lived assets such as utilities, transport or communications networks. Returns depend on utilisation, contracts, regulation, operating costs and financing rather than the asset's physical durability alone. Contracted revenue can reduce uncertainty while introducing counterparty and renewal risks.~~Construction-stage and operating-stage assets have different risk profiles. Inflation-linked pricing may be delayed, capped or offset by inflation in costs.|Greenfield: Development and construction exposure.;;Brownfield: Existing operating assets.;;Contracted/merchant: Revenue fixed by agreement or exposed to market demand.|An operating asset earns ₹15 lakh and spends ₹5 on operations, with ₹6 debt service.~~Cash available before debt service is ₹10.;;Coverage is 10 ÷ 6 = 1.67×.;;A ₹4 revenue decline reduces coverage to 1× if costs are fixed.~~Revenue stability supports debt capacity.~~Maintenance, taxes and contract definitions require further adjustments.|Identify demand and contract risks.;;Separate construction from operation.;;Stress maintenance and refinancing.
T23.15|Collectibles and other stores of value may be held for enjoyment, scarcity or expected appreciation. Their valuation often depends on condition, provenance, buyer demand and infrequent comparable transactions. They generally lack a contractual cash-flow stream to anchor value.~~Quoted auction prices may omit fees and costs; selling can take time. Authenticity, custody and insurance are central economic concerns rather than administrative details.|Collectibles: Art, rare objects and similar assets.;;Storage assets: Holdings requiring secure custody.;;Non-income value: Return driven mainly by resale price.|An item costs ₹10 lakh, storage and insurance total ₹1, and eventual sale is ₹13 with a ₹1 selling charge.~~Total outlay is ₹11.;;Net sale proceeds are ₹12.;;Net gain is ₹1 before tax and time-value effects.~~Headline appreciation overstates the economic gain.~~Illiquidity can make the sale price and timing uncertain.|Verify authenticity and ownership.;;Include all holding and sale costs.;;Avoid assuming continuous market liquidity.
T23.16|Private-fund fees commonly combine management charges and performance participation, called carried interest. A distribution waterfall determines who receives cash and when, including capital repayment, preferred return, catch-up and sharing rules where applicable. Terms vary; no single formula describes every fund.~~Timing and calculation base matter. Whole-fund and deal-level waterfalls can distribute carry at different times, making clawback and escrow protections relevant.|Management fee: Charge on a stated capital or asset base.;;Carry: Contractual share of eligible profit.;;Waterfall: Ordered allocation of cash among investors and manager.|Assume ₹100 capital, ₹150 proceeds and 20% carry on profit after returning capital, with no other fees or hurdle.~~Return ₹100 capital first.;;Profit is ₹50 and carry is ₹10.;;Investor receives ₹140 in total.~~Net multiple is 1.4×, not the gross 1.5×.~~Real agreements may contain additional tiers and different bases.|Read every distribution tier.;;Model fees through time.;;Review clawback enforceability.
T23.17|A commitment is a contractual promise to provide capital; a capital call requests part of it. Unfunded commitments remain future liquidity needs even when current invested assets look small. The J-curve describes a possible pattern of early costs and later gains or distributions, not a guaranteed recovery.~~Investors must manage calls and distributions together. A fund's reported NAV does not automatically provide cash to meet another call.|Committed capital: Total promised amount.;;Called capital: Amount requested and funded.;;Unfunded commitment: Remaining callable amount.|An investor commits ₹100 lakh, has funded ₹30 and holds only ₹20 liquid reserves.~~Unfunded commitment is ₹70.;;A new ₹25 call exceeds reserves by ₹5.;;Plan additional liquidity rather than treating NAV as readily spendable.~~Commitment management is a financing problem.~~Call timing and penalties depend on the agreement.|Track all outstanding commitments.;;Stress overlapping calls.;;Distinguish distributions from reported gains.
T23.18|Private-investment performance combines cash-flow timing and multiples. IRR is the discount rate equating dated contributions and distributions with terminal value. MOIC compares total value with investment; DPI shows distributed value, RVPI residual value, and TVPI their sum using a consistent paid-in denominator.~~IRR can be influenced by financing and early distributions. Residual NAV is estimated, so high TVPI does not mean all gains are realised.|DPI: Distributions divided by paid-in capital.;;RVPI: Residual NAV divided by paid-in capital.;;TVPI: Distributed plus residual value divided by paid-in capital.|Paid-in capital is ₹100, distributions ₹40 and residual NAV ₹90.~~DPI = 0.4×.;;RVPI = 0.9× and TVPI = 1.3×.;;IRR needs the dates of contributions and receipts.~~Only ₹40 has actually been distributed.~~State whether metrics are gross or net and use compatible denominators.|Inspect dated cash flows.;;Separate realised and residual value.;;Compare net metrics and valuation policies.
T23.19|Private-asset analysis faces infrequent transactions, estimated valuations and selective performance histories. Stale marks can make volatility look low without reducing economic risk. Survivorship bias appears when failed or unavailable funds are omitted from the comparison sample.~~Liquidity risk is the inability to sell at an acceptable price when needed. Lockups, transfer restrictions and discounts can matter even for a financially healthy asset.|Valuation smoothing: Slow recognition of changing value.;;Liquidity discount: Price concession for an available exit.;;Survivorship bias: Results exclude missing or failed participants.|A portfolio reports NAV ₹100 lakh; a secondary buyer offers ₹75 when the investor needs immediate cash.~~Quoted exit discount is 25%.;;The recorded NAV cannot meet a ₹90 obligation at that sale price.;;Investigate valuation date and sale restrictions.~~Reported value and accessible liquidity are separate.~~An offer is not proof of fundamental value, but is relevant to funding capacity.|Review valuation evidence.;;Include missing and failed funds.;;Stress discounts and sale delays.
